    "lower6-maths-differentiation-e12"
   ],
   "selfCheck": [
    "lower6-maths-stationary-points-q1",
    "lower6-maths-stationary-points-q2",
    "lower6-maths-stationary-points-q3",
    "lower6-maths-stationary-points-q4",
    "lower6-maths-stationary-points-q5"
   ],
   "reviewNotes": [
    "Concavity/inflexion treated only briefly.",
    "Syllabus references, scope and level of difficulty are to be checked against the official text; all facts, formulas and worked examples to be verified by a teacher before release."
   ],
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "key",
     "style": "definition",
     "title": "Stationary points",
     "md": "A stationary point is where $\\frac{dy}{dx}=0$. It is classified by the second derivative: $\\frac{d^2y}{dx^2}<0$ gives a **maximum**, $>0$ a **minimum**, $=0$ is inconclusive (check the sign of $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ on each side). A curve is increasing where $\\frac{dy}{dx}>0$."
    },
    {
     "type": "key",
     "style": "methode",
     "title": "Optimisation",
     "md": "1. Draw a diagram and name the variables. 2. Write the quantity to optimise as a function of ONE variable (use the given constraint). 3. Differentiate and set to 0. 4. Prove it is a maximum/minimum. 5. Answer the question with units."
    },
    {
     "type": "key",
     "style": "pieges",
     "title": "Common mistakes",
     "md": "- Stopping once $x$ is found: also find $y$ and the nature of the point.\n- $\\frac{dy}{dx}=0$ does not always give a max or min: $y=x^3$ has a point of inflexion at $x=0$.\n- Forgetting to reject values outside the sensible domain (for example a negative length)."
    },
    {
     "type": "example",
     "title": "Example 1",
     "statement": "Find and classify the stationary points of $y=x^3-6x^2+9x+2$.",
     "steps": [
      "$\\frac{dy}{dx}=3x^2-12x+9=3(x-1)(x-3)=0$, so $x=1$ or $x=3$. Then $y(1)=6$, $y(3)=2$.",
      "$\\frac{d^2y}{dx^2}=6x-12$: at $x=1$ it is $-6<0$ (maximum); at $x=3$ it is $6>0$ (minimum)."
     ],
     "answer": "Maximum $(1,6)$, minimum $(3,2)$",
     "figure": {
      "kind": "plot",
      "xmin": -0.5,
      "xmax": 4.5,
      "ymin": -5,
      "ymax": 13,
      "grid": 1,
      "w": 400,
      "h": 260,
      "curves": [
       {
        "expr": "x^3-6x^2+9x+2",
        "color": "blue"
       }
      ],
      "points": [
       {
        "x": 1,
        "y": 6,
        "label": "max (1, 6)",
        "color": "red"
       },
       {
        "x": 3,
        "y": 2,
        "label": "min (3, 2)",
        "color": "green"
       }
      ],
      "segments": [],
      "xlabel": "x"
     }
    },
    {
     "type": "example",
     "title": "Example 2",
     "statement": "Squares of side $x$ cm are cut from the corners of a 12 cm square metal sheet; the sides are folded up to make an open box. Find the $x$ that maximises the volume.",
     "steps": [
      "Base side $12-2x$, height $x$: $V=x(12-2x)^2$ for $0<x<6$.",
      "$\\frac{dV}{dx}=(12-2x)^2-4x(12-2x)=(12-2x)(12-6x)=0$, so $x=2$ ($x=6$ is rejected: no box).",
      "$V(2)=2\\times64=128$; check $V(1)=100$ and $V(3)=108$, both smaller, so a maximum."
     ],
     "answer": "$x=2$ cm, $V_{max}=128$ cm³"
    }
   ],
